1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,270
For most people a library is a place where you must be quiet.

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:06,828
For Oliver Sacks, it was the place where he became himself.

3
00:00:07,033 --> 00:00:19,277
His essay is a warm memory of every library he loved, and then, in its last two paragraphs, a shock: the shelves are being emptied, because students now find everything on a screen.

4
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:31,576
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 13: On Libraries, an essay by Oliver Sacks.

5
00:00:33,967 --> 00:00:35,075
Six parts.

6
00:00:35,075 --> 00:00:36,417
Oliver Sacks.

7
00:00:36,417 --> 00:00:37,784
The library at home.

8
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:40,271
Willesden, where he was a good learner.

9
00:00:40,271 --> 00:00:42,875
Science, Oxford and the vaults.

10
00:00:42,875 --> 00:00:45,518
New York: space and strangers.

11
00:00:45,518 --> 00:00:46,627
And the empty shelves.

12
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:56,450
Oliver Sacks was born in London in nineteen thirty three, the son of two doctors.

13
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:03,781
He studied medicine at Oxford and from nineteen sixty five worked as a neurologist in New York for nearly fifty years.

14
00:01:03,781 --> 00:01:11,333
He became famous for writing about his patients as human beings rather than cases, in books like Awakenings.

15
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:14,784
The New York Times called him the poet laureate of medicine.

16
00:01:14,784 --> 00:01:20,448
This is a personal essay: first person, memories that move towards an opinion.

17
00:01:20,448 --> 00:01:26,683
The textbook calls it a praise of intellectual freedom, community, and the joy of lucky discovery.

18
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:36,469
The essay has thirteen short paragraphs in three movements: childhood and youth, adult life in New York, and then the turn.

19
00:01:36,469 --> 00:01:43,084
Eleven paragraphs of gratitude, then two of loss, and nothing warns you that the turn is coming.

20
00:01:43,084 --> 00:01:44,880
That is why it hits so hard.

21
00:01:49,667 --> 00:01:56,296
His favourite room as a child was the family library: oak panelled, every wall lined with books.

22
00:01:56,296 --> 00:02:00,541
His father's Hebrew scholarship and all of Ibsen's plays.

23
00:02:00,541 --> 00:02:03,848
The young poets of the Great War, many of them killed.

24
00:02:03,848 --> 00:02:14,252
On the lowest shelves, where a small boy could reach, his older brothers' adventure books, including Kipling's Jungle Book: he identified with the wolf boy Mowgli.

25
00:02:14,252 --> 00:02:16,335
His mother's Dickens and Shaw.

26
00:02:16,335 --> 00:02:20,697
Medical books in a locked cabinet, with the key left in the door.

27
00:02:20,697 --> 00:02:26,252
He learned to read at three or four, and when he was late for a meal, the family knew where to look.

28
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:37,128
The library that mattered most was not at home but Willesden Public Library, five minutes' walk away.

29
00:02:37,128 --> 00:02:45,201
He calls it his Ur library: Ur is a German prefix meaning the original, the first of all libraries.

30
00:02:45,201 --> 00:02:48,925
There, he says, he received his real education.

31
00:02:48,925 --> 00:02:54,511
He disliked school; in class, information went in one ear and out the other.

32
00:02:54,511 --> 00:02:56,178
He could not learn passively.

33
00:02:56,178 --> 00:02:59,108
He was not a good pupil, but a good learner.

34
00:02:59,108 --> 00:03:10,086
In the library he roamed the shelves freely, followed what fascinated him, became himself, and enjoyed the quiet companionship of other readers on quests of their own.

35
00:03:14,867 --> 00:03:19,048
As he grew, his reading leaned to astronomy and chemistry.

36
00:03:19,048 --> 00:03:31,887
His school's Walker Library was excellent in history but weak in science, so a teacher wrote him a testimonial that got him into the Science Museum library, where he devoured huge chemistry treatises.

37
00:03:31,887 --> 00:03:38,280
At Oxford he had the Radcliffe Science Library and the Bodleian, which goes back to sixteen oh two.

38
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:48,384
There he stumbled on Theodore Hook, a forgotten nineteenth century wit said to have improvised more than five hundred operas, and decided to write his life.

39
00:03:48,384 --> 00:03:54,027
The lesson: one library cannot hold everything; a hungry reader goes looking.

40
00:03:55,233 --> 00:04:00,469
But the Oxford library he loved most was his own college's, the Queen's College.

41
00:04:00,469 --> 00:04:03,750
Above ground, a building by Christopher Wren.

42
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:09,792
Beneath it, in an underground maze of heating pipes and shelves, the catacombs.

43
00:04:09,792 --> 00:04:21,055
There he held incunabula, books printed before fifteen oh one, a sixteenth century book of animals with Dürer's rhinoceros, and all of Darwin's works in first editions.

44
00:04:21,055 --> 00:04:28,425
He fell in love with the magnificent language of Sir Thomas Browne, and met Johnson, Hume, Pope and Swift.

45
00:04:28,425 --> 00:04:32,292
And they were not locked away; they were just sitting on the shelves.

46
00:04:32,292 --> 00:04:36,106
There, he says, he gained a sense of history and of his own language.

47
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:47,959
In nineteen sixty five Sacks arrived in New York, in a horrid, pokey little apartment with nowhere to write.

48
00:04:47,959 --> 00:04:51,761
He wrote part of his first book balanced on top of the refrigerator.

49
00:04:51,761 --> 00:05:04,300
He longed for space, and found it in the library of the Albert Einstein College of Medicine, where he worked: a large table to write at, and stacks to wander with no idea what his eye might fall on.

50
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:07,347
Unexpected treasures, lucky finds.

51
00:05:07,347 --> 00:05:11,572
That is serendipity: the joy of finding something valuable by accident.

52
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:14,013
And there were people.

53
00:05:14,013 --> 00:05:27,502
The library was quiet, yet whispered conversations might start in the stacks, two readers hunting the same bound volume of the journal Brain from eighteen ninety, and those conversations could become friendships.

54
00:05:27,502 --> 00:05:33,830
Everyone was absorbed in a private world, and yet there was a sense of community, even intimacy.

55
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:42,453
Part of it came from the physical book itself: handled, passed between readers, carrying the names and dates of earlier borrowers.

56
00:05:47,233 --> 00:05:48,198
Then the turn.

57
00:05:48,198 --> 00:05:50,998
But a shift was occurring by the nineteen nineties.

58
00:05:50,998 --> 00:05:58,354
Students ignored the shelves and found what they needed on computers, so the college decided to get rid of the books.

59
00:05:58,354 --> 00:06:08,498
Visiting a couple of months ago, he was horrified: the once overflowing shelves were nearly empty, the books thrown out with remarkably little objection.

60
00:06:08,498 --> 00:06:13,697
It felt like a murder, a crime, the destruction of centuries of knowledge.

61
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:20,201
A librarian reassures him that everything of worth has been digitised.

62
00:06:20,201 --> 00:06:21,803
He is not reassured.

63
00:06:21,803 --> 00:06:29,485
He does not use a computer, and there is something irreplaceable about a physical book: its look, its smell, its heft.

64
00:06:29,485 --> 00:06:41,113
Then the last memory closes the circle: in nineteen sixty seven, rummaging in the stacks, he found an eighteen seventy three book on migraine, which inspired his own first book.

65
00:06:41,113 --> 00:06:44,524
Digitise the shelf, and you may keep the text.

66
00:06:44,524 --> 00:06:46,234
But you lose the lucky accident.

67
00:06:47,467 --> 00:07:02,039
For the long question, traditional against digital: browsing and lucky finds against searching for what you already know you want; the physical book against text on a screen; a community of strangers against each reader alone.

68
00:07:02,039 --> 00:07:07,319
Notice Sacks's tone: not anger at technology, but sorrow for what is lost.

69
00:07:09,733 --> 00:07:10,504
Pause and think.

70
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:23,391
Ur- means original: the first library, where he got his "real education".

71
00:07:25,367 --> 00:07:25,802
One more.

72
00:07:33,433 --> 00:07:37,862
He could not learn passively in class, but learned actively among the shelves.

73
00:07:40,033 --> 00:07:41,115
Quick recap.

74
00:07:41,115 --> 00:07:43,368
Sacks, and a personal essay.

75
00:07:43,368 --> 00:07:46,858
The library at home and the Ur library at Willesden.

76
00:07:46,858 --> 00:07:48,537
The vaults under Oxford.

77
00:07:48,537 --> 00:07:50,608
Space and strangers in New York.

78
00:07:50,608 --> 00:07:52,318
And the shock of the empty shelves.

79
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,025
That was On Libraries.

80
00:07:58,025 --> 00:08:01,606
Next essay: Marriage as a Social Institution.

81
00:08:01,606 --> 00:08:02,163
See you there.

